  "reasoning": "The claim that monosodium glutamate (MSG) causes headaches, numbness, and other symptoms collectively referred to as \"Chinese restaurant syndrome\" is false. The concept originated from an anecdotal 1968 letter to the New England Journal of Medicine, which led to widespread public panic and racially biased perceptions. Decades of subsequent rigorous, double-blind, placebo-controlled studies have consistently failed to demonstrate a causal link between dietary MSG consumption and these symptoms. Major scientific and regulatory bodies worldwide, including the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the World Health Organization (WHO), classify MSG as \"generally recognized as safe\" (GRAS). Furthermore, chemically, the body metabolizes added glutamate in MSG in the exact same manner as naturally occurring glutamate found abundantly in foods like tomatoes, mushrooms, and aged cheeses.",
